        <body>
            <listPerson>
                <person xml:id="MAC06" sex="1">
                    <persName>
                        <roleName/>
                        <forename>William Charles</forename>
                        <surname>Macready</surname>
                        <addName/>
                    </persName>
                    <birth when="1793-03-03">
                        <placeName> </placeName>
                    </birth>
                    <death when="1873-04-27"/>
                    <occupation>actor</occupation>
                    <occupation>theatre manager</occupation>
                    <note type="editorial">
                        <p>Macready was a successful tragic actor and theatre manager at both Drury Lane and Covent Garden.</p>
                        <p>Most of the contacts could conceivably be referring instead to Macready's father, William Macready 
  (1755-1829), also an actor and theatre manager. But there are a few meetings after his death without 
  differentiation and the entry on 21 March 1821, 'M and T T call, and Macready (Ric. III):', indicates the younger 
  Macready, who acted in the Covent Garden production of Richard III that restored much of the original 
  text in the same month.</p>
                    </note>
                    <bibl type="other">
                        <title>DNB</title>
                    </bibl>
                    <bibl type="journalism"><title>Examiner</title>, 11 March 1821</bibl>
                    <bibl type="printedbook"><title>The Diaries of William Charles 
  Macready, 1833-1851</title>, ed. by William Toynbee (London: Chapman and Hall Ltd, 1912)</bibl>
                </person>
            </listPerson>
